WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
In Sim, workflows are the repeatable
processes that power your workspace's AI

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.260
systems. They're where your business's
logic, automations, and agents are

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.520
defined, where data and resources get
converted into action. Almost everything

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.140
you'll make will use them. Whether it's a
support agent that triages tickets, or a

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.940
pipeline that enriches your leads, or a
monitoring process that runs on a

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.840
schedule, these functions are all
expressed through workflows. Let's open

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.600
one up. This is a workflow, a visual
program, a process laid out as connected

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.100
blocks, each one a step. Here it is,
running once, start to finish.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.380
Some blocks can act as triggers, which
start the workflow. And Sim offers

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:51.540
thousands of integrations with other
services, a library of third -party

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.800
actions and triggers that's actively
maintained and always growing. Each block

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.260
is configured by its parameters. Open one
up and you can see them. The operation it

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.200
performs, the query it searches for, and
how many results to bring back.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:13.100
A parameter can hold a fixed value, or use
the result of a previous block via a

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.400
connection tag. The answer's message
inserts the excerpts that retrieve context

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.980
found. A connection tag names the block
and the field, enclosed in angle brackets,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.840
and points right at that block's output.
You can follow any tag back to where its

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.040
value came from. This one reads from the
block right beside it, and this one

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.500
reaches much further back.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.020
When it runs, each block starts the moment
the blocks feeding it are done. So once

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.640
retrieve context returns, the answer and
the log both run at the same time.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.380
And when several feed into one, it waits
for all of them. The log finishes

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:56.000
instantly, but the result holds until the
answer is done too, then runs once with

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.000
both ready.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.980
That's the whole model. A trigger starts
it. Each block runs as soon as its inputs

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.920
arrive. Data moves through connection
tags, and the run ends at the result.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.960
And the same rules apply no matter how big
a workflow gets. Add more blocks, more

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.760
branches, loops, even whole sub workflows
nested inside. And it all still runs the

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.760
same way.
